  "image_name": "45a114e38ffb90e8b38fb1b3fc8082ca.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts today’s fossil-fuel-based chemical synthesis with an alternative circular route based on electrosynthesis. On the left, fossil fuels are burned, generating flue gas and feeding industrial processing that produces chemicals, fuels, plastics, and other commodities. This is presented as the conventional linear pathway.\n\nThe central and right portions show a low-carbon pathway in which CO₂ is first captured either from concentrated point sources such as flue gas or directly from the atmosphere. The captured CO₂ is then sent to an electrolyser stack for CO₂ electrolysis. This step appears to convert CO₂ back into useful products, linking carbon capture to manufacturing rather than relying on fossil feedstocks.\n\nA second major theme is circularity of plastic or material waste. Products can go through reuse, then disintegration, classification, cycling, and recasting, with material streams fed back toward the electrolysis-based production loop. This suggests a system that combines recycling with electrochemical conversion.\n\nThe figure also highlights enabling requirements for the electrosynthesis route, including renewable energy, water, raw materials, and land. Overall, it depicts a transition from a fossil-derived, emission-generating process to a more circular, CO₂-utilizing production system.",
